aptured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"74f6814f-8ccf-42bc-aee0-c087dea07887","version":"2.1.280","gitBranch":"main"}
