{"parentUuid":"cd8be942-c5cd-467a-a4f5-51a76806f9a4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"87d6c547-b2ad-4d4f-b4f7-18e6b02e26a7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XkNbNN44bfEcKoimZ6mvBq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# STATUS\n2\t\n3\t## Done\n4\t- Repo brought under git and pushed to erash11 (2026-09-09).\n5\t- All 12 PFF API specs saved to `docs/swagger/` (2026-09-13). Eight were new; master-data\n6\t  and grades-feeds were refreshed (both only gained endpoints). The key downloads specs\n7\t  directly: `GET /doc/swagger/<slug>.yaml` with the Bearer JWT.\n8\t- PFF EPA located and verified (2026-09-13): `/v1/video/ncaa/games/{id}/plays` carries\n9\t  `expected_points_added` and `expected_points_added_legacy`, 100% play coverage on\n10\t  Baylor 2024-2026 and six random non-Baylor 2025 games.\n11\t- Video plays ingested for all 25 Baylor 2024-2025 games (2026-09-13): `pff_premium`\n12\t  category `video_plays`, 4,517 rows (both offences), zero missing EPA.\n13\t  `scripts/pull_video_plays.py --season 2024 --season 2025`. Season and week come\n14\t  from the games table: a bowl game's rows carry week \"BG\". Pre-change backup:\n15\t  `data/pff_before_video_plays_20260913_204343.db`.\n16\t- Full 2024 and 2025 seasons pulled (2026-09-13): 285,070 and 290,427 plays plus the\n17\t  games lists, `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py`. Verified against the per-game pull\n18\t  (all 2,215 Baylor plays identical). Current equals legacy on 1.7% of plays, so the\n19\t  2024 model backfill happened. Consumed by PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA Phase 7.\n20\t\n21\t- EPA source comparison done 2026-09-13 in PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA (Phases 6 and 7).\n22\t  Decision (Eric): EPA (PFF current) is the EPA source going forward.\n23\t\n24\t## Next\n25\t1. Pull 2026 weekly with `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py --season 2026 --refresh`, so\n26\t   MatchupAnalysis opponent profiles can quote EPA (PFF current) instead of CFBD PPA.\n27\t2. After the 2026 season, a full 2026 pull for the PlayCalls Phase 7 rerun.\n28\t\n29\t## Open questions\n30\t- None waiting on Eric.\n31\t\n32\t## Gotchas\n33\t- The season feed is about 2.5 GB decoded; stream it (ijson), never `response.json()`.\n34\t- `season_plays.to_frame` needs `dtype=object`, or gappy int columns land as `\"2.0\"`/`\"nan\"`.\n35\t- Bowl-week rows carry week \"BG\"; take season and week from the games list.\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## Merged from windows copy (2026-09-11)\n40\t\n41\t<!-- Contents of STATUS.md.from-windows, kept verbatim so nothing is lost. Fold into the sections above when convenient. -->\n42\t\n43\t# STATUS\n44\t\n45\tRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n46\t\n47\t## Current state\n48\t- Snapshot committed 2026-09-09 during migration to the DEV + GitHub workflow.\n49\t\n50\t## Next\n51\t-\n52\t\n53\t## Open questions\n54\t-\n55\t\n56\t## Gotchas\n57\t-\n58\t"}]},"uuid":"a5474a38-0373-48ee-8c20-7b6cff82df58","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:51:44.676Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/docs/STATUS.md","content":"# STATUS\n\n## Done\n- Repo brought under git and pushed to erash11 (2026-09-09).\n- All 12 PFF API specs saved to `docs/swagger/` (2026-09-13). Eight were new; master-data\n  and grades-feeds were refreshed (both only gained endpoints). The key downloads specs\n  directly: `GET /doc/swagger/<slug>.yaml` with the Bearer JWT.\n- PFF EPA located and verified (2026-09-13): `/v1/video/ncaa/games/{id}/plays` carries\n  `expected_points_added` and `expected_points_added_legacy`, 100% play coverage on\n  Baylor 2024-2026 and six random non-Baylor 2025 games.\n- Video plays ingested for all 25 Baylor 2024-2025 games (2026-09-13): `pff_premium`\n  category `video_plays`, 4,517 rows (both offences), zero missing EPA.\n  `scripts/pull_video_plays.py --season 2024 --season 2025`. Season and week come\n  from the games table: a bowl game's rows carry week \"BG\". Pre-change backup:\n  `data/pff_before_video_plays_20260913_204343.db`.\n- Full 2024 and 2025 seasons pulled (2026-09-13): 285,070 and 290,427 plays plus the\n  games lists, `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py`. Verified against the per-game pull\n  (all 2,215 Baylor plays identical). Current equals legacy on 1.7% of plays, so the\n  2024 model backfill happened. Consumed by PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA Phase 7.\n\n- EPA source comparison done 2026-09-13 in PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA (Phases 6 and 7).\n  Decision (Eric): EPA (PFF current) is the EPA source going forward.\n\n## Next\n1. Pull 2026 weekly with `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py --season 2026 --refresh`, so\n   MatchupAnalysis opponent profiles can quote EPA (PFF current) instead of CFBD PPA.\n2. After the 2026 season, a full 2026 pull for the PlayCalls Phase 7 rerun.\n\n## Open questions\n- None waiting on Eric.\n\n## Gotchas\n- The season feed is about 2.5 GB decoded; stream it (ijson), never `response.json()`.\n- `season_plays.to_frame` needs `dtype=object`, or gappy int columns land as `\"2.0\"`/`\"nan\"`.\n- Bowl-week rows carry week \"BG\"; take season and week from the games list.\n\n---\n\n## Merged from windows copy (2026-09-11)\n\n<!-- Contents of STATUS.md.from-windows, kept verbatim so nothing is lost. Fold into the sections above when convenient. -->\n\n# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state\n- Snapshot committed 2026-09-09 during migration to the DEV + GitHub workflow.\n\n## Next\n-\n\n## Open questions\n-\n\n## Gotchas\n-\n","numLines":58,"startLine":1,"totalLines":58}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cd8be942-c5cd-467a-a4f5-51a76806f9a4","session_id":"74f6814f-8ccf-42bc-aee0-c087dea07887","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project","sessionId":"74f6814f-8ccf-42bc-aee0-c087dea07887","version":"2.1.280","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"a5474a38-0373-48ee-8c20-7b6cff82df58","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"nested_memory","path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/CLAUDE.md","content":{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# PFF Data Project — Agent Guide\n\nThis file is the canonical project memory for any AI coding agent working in this\nrepo. It is mirrored verbatim into `AGENTS.md` so OpenAI Codex (and any other\nagent that reads `AGENTS.md`) gets the same context. A pre-commit hook in\n`.githooks/pre-commit` blocks commits where the two files diverge.\n\nIf you edit one, edit both. See \"Keeping CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md in sync\" below.\n\n---\n\n## What this project is\n\nA standalone Python data pipeline that pulls Pro Football Focus (PFF) game and\nplayer data into a local **DuckDB** database for Baylor University Football\nanalysis. Scope is **Baylor + every Big 12 team + non-conference opponents** for\nthe **current season plus two prior**.\n\nThe DB *is* the deliverable. There are no dashboards in this repo. Downstream\nconsumers — Power BI, Jupyter notebooks, and a future athlete management system\n(AMS) project that will unify PFF with Catapult GPS, VALD ForceDecks, and Perch\nVBT data — own visualization and cross-domain joins.\n\n## Sister pipelines (do not modify from here)\n\n- **VALD ForceDecks (CMJ/IMTP):** `..\\..\\ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` — the\n  architectural template this project mirrors.\n- **Catapult GPS:** `..\\..\\DataBase_GPS_Reporting`\n- **Perch VBT:** not yet built.\n\nThe pattern across all of them is: per-domain Python pipeline → per-domain DB →\nfuture AMS layer unions them on `(athlete_id, source_system, pulled_at)`.\n\n## Tech stack\n\n- Python 3.12+\n- `duckdb`, `requests`, `python-dotenv`, `PyJWT`, `pyyaml`, `pandas`, `pytest`\n- No web framework, no ORM, no dashboard libs — keep it lean\n\n## Layout\n\n```\nPFF_Data_Project/\n├── CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md       # this file (mirrored)\n├── README.md                   # user-facing setup and usage\n├── requirements.txt\n├── .env.example                # PFF_BASE_URL, PFF_API_KEY, BAYLOR_TEAM_ID\n├── .gitignore\n├── .githooks/pre-commit        # blocks CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md divergence\n├── run_pipeline.py             # CLI entry\n├── config/\n│   └── pipeline.yaml           # team/season scope, endpoints, retry config\n├── src/\n│   ├── db.py                   # DuckDB schema + connect + backup helpers\n│   ├── ingest/\n│   │   ├── pff_client.py       # PFF HTTP client (stub until Phase 0 done)\n│   │   └── pipeline.py         # Orchestration: dims → facts → log\n│   └── shape/transform.py      # JSON → tidy rows (teams/games/grades/stats/premium)\n├── data/                       # small extracts only; the DuckDB lives at $PFF_DB\n├── docs/\n│   └── pff_api_notes.md        # Phase 0 spike output\n├── scripts/\n│   └── phase0_probe.py         # Credential discovery probe\n└── tests/\n    ├── test_db.py\n    └── fixtures/\n```\n\n## Data model contract\n\nDimensions are stable identities; facts are timestamped measurements.\n\n- **Dimensions:** `teams`, `players`, `player_seasons`, `games`\n- **Facts:** `pff_grades` (per-player per-game, long form), `pff_stats` (extended\n  stats, long form), `pff_premium` (advanced metrics, keyed JSON passthrough)\n- **Operational:** `ingest_log`\n\n`players` / `player_seasons` are derived from the grade rows (which carry\nplayer_id, name, position, team_id, season) — cleanest \"who actually played\"\nsource; avoids the giant all-NCAA `/players` pull.\n\nEvery fact row carries `source_system = 'PFF'` and `pulled_at`. This is\nnon-negotiable — it is the contract the future AMS layer relies on to union PFF\nwith Catapult, VALD, and Perch facts.\n\n`pff_player_id` is the durable join key. This pipeline does **not** maintain a\ncanonical athlete master record across systems. That mapping\n(`roster.athlete_id → pff_player_id`) belongs to the future AMS project. Do not\ninvent it here.\n\n## API contract (Phase 0 complete)\n\nFull contract is in `docs/pff_api_notes.md`; OpenAPI specs in `docs/swagger/`.\nEssentials:\n\n- **Auth:** `POST /auth/login` with header `x-api-key: <key>` → `{\"jwt\"}`. Send\n  `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` on all calls. JWT lasts ~1h; re-auth on 401.\n  Rate-limited (HTTP 429) — client backs off. `src/ingest/pff_client.py` owns this.\n- **Base:** `https://api.profootballfocus.com`, league slug `ncaa`, season = year.\n- **Baylor:** team_id / franchise_id = `118` (`BAYLOR_TEAM_ID` in `.env`).\n- **Scope is open:** the key reads all NCAA teams + grades for any game, so\n  Baylor + Big 12 + opponents is achievable despite the token's `franchise:118`\n  constraint.\n- **Play-by-play exists in the Video Feeds API** (EPA fields confirmed 2026-09-13):\n  `/v1/video/ncaa/games/{game_id}/plays`, 330+ fields per play, including PFF EPA\n  (`expected_points_added` and `expected_points_added_legacy`). Ingested for Baylor\n  2024-2025 into `pff_premium` as category `video_plays` by\n  `scripts/pull_video_plays.py`; deliberately not in `PREMIUM_CATEGORIES` (the\n  season loop would pull every scoped game at ~1.5 MB each).\n- **Full seasons stream from `/v1/video/ncaa/{season}/plays`** (~2.5 GB decoded,\n  about a minute): `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py --season YYYY` writes\n  `data/video_plays_slim_<season>.parquet` (31 fields per play) and\n  `data/video_games_<season>.parquet` (final scores; the plays feed only has the\n  score before each snap). Both gitignored. `season_plays.to_frame` must build\n  the frame with `dtype=object`: without it an int column with a gap becomes\n  float64 and is stored as the text `\"2.0\"` and `\"nan\"`.\n  Premium feeds (`/v1/premium/...`) provide play-level advanced metrics → captured\n  in `pff_premium` as keyed JSON passthrough. All 12 API specs are in `docs/swagger/`.\n- **FUTURE:** player-tracking feeds are deliberately not modeled in v1.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Credentials never in code.** `.env` only. Loaded via `python-dotenv`. The\n  `.env` file is gitignored; `.env.example` is committed as the template.\n- **DuckDB upserts:** use `INSERT OR REPLACE INTO` (idempotent refresh — re-pulling\n  a week updates values rather than duplicating). See `_upsert` in `pipeline.py`.\n- **Config path resolution:** always anchor to `Path(__file__).resolve().parent`\n  walks, never relative paths from CWD. `run_pipeline.py` and `src/db.py` show\n  the pattern.\n- **The DB is not in the repo.** `src/db.py` resolves it from **`$PFF_DB`**, falling\n  back to the old `data/pff.db` when the variable is unset. Windows:\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\pff-data\\pff.db` (set 2026-09-22). Reason: `~/Desktop/DEV` is a\n  two-way Google Drive mirror, and Drive re-uploads and **locks** a 500 MB file on\n  every write; on 2026-09-22 it held the file open against a running pull\n  (`GoogleDriveFS.exe`). Keep the DB, its WAL and its backups out of the mirrored tree.\n- **Backups before destructive ops:** copy the DB to\n  `pff_before_<operation>_<YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS>.db` **beside it**, not into `data/`.\n  `src/db.backup_db()` does this — use it from any new destructive code path.\n- **Idempotent ingests:** running the same week twice must be a no-op.\n- **One concern per module.** Client does HTTP. Shape does JSON-to-rows. Pipeline\n  orchestrates. db.py owns the schema. Don't blur these.\n- **Tests use mocked HTTP**, not live API calls. The Phase 0 probe is the only\n  script that hits the real API.\n- **No comments that narrate the obvious.** Comments are for non-obvious WHYs\n  (e.g., \"DuckDB requires ON CONFLICT here; SQLite syntax silently fails\").\n\n## CLI commands\n\n```\npython run_pipeline.py --dry-run               # auth + scope check, no writes\npython run_pipeline.py --backfill              # full 3-season pull\npython run_pipeline.py --season 2025           # full season\npython run_pipeline.py --season 2025 --week 8  # one week\npython run_pipeline.py --diagnose [NAME]       # query local DB\npython run_pipeline.py --reset-db              # backup + reinit\n```\n\nMoving data between the Mac and Windows (never re-pull what the other machine already has):\n\n```\npython scripts/export_snapshot.py              # every season -> Parquet on Drive\npython scripts/export_snapshot.py --season 2026\npython scripts/load_snapshot.py --dry-run      # what would change here\npython scripts/load_snapshot.py                # load what Drive has\n```\n\n## Moving data between machines\n\nThe DB never travels. `git` cannot hold a 600 MB binary and a live database inside a\nsynced folder gets locked and re-uploaded on every write. Instead each machine keeps its\nown local DB and they exchange **immutable Parquet snapshots** through Google Drive:\nwritten once, never mutated, so a half-synced file cannot corrupt anything.\n\n- Root: `$PFF_SNAPSHOT_DIR`, else `.../00_Primary_Storage_Drive/pff-snapshots`.\n- One file per table per season (`pff_grades/season=2026.parquet`), so a single season\n  moves without dragging six million rows behind it. It is small: all of 2026,\n  1.33 M rows, is about 6 MB.\n- `manifest.json` records rows, bytes, which machine wrote it and when. An export\n  merges into it, so exporting one season never erases the record of the others.\n- The load is season-scoped and idempotent: it deletes and reinserts only the seasons\n  the snapshot carries, leaves the rest alone, and backs the DB up first.\n- `ingest_log` is never exported. It says what **this** machine fetched; copying it\n  would make the other machine's history a lie.\n- After `dev-sync` pulls the scripts, run `load_snapshot.py` to pull the data. Git\n  carries the code, Drive carries the rows.\n\n## Testing\n\n```\npytest tests/\n```\n\nTests that need a DB use a per-test temp path; don't touch `data/pff.db`.\n\n## Keeping CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md in sync\n\n`AGENTS.md` is a byte-identical copy of `CLAUDE.md`. The pre-commit hook at\n`.githooks/pre-commit` blocks commits when they differ. Activate the hook once\nper clone:\n\n```\ngit config core.hooksPath .githooks\n```\n\nWhen updating agent guidance, edit `CLAUDE.md` first, then:\n\n```\ncp CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md\ngit add CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md\n```\n\nIf you intentionally need them to diverge (tool-specific instructions for one\nagent), update the hook before committing — don't bypass it silently with\n`--no-verify`.\n\n## Working with this repo from Codex\n\nCodex reads `AGENTS.md` automatically. Everything above applies to Codex\nsessions identically. Codex-specific notes, if any are ever needed, go in a\nseparate section here and get mirrored.\n\n\n## Session routine\n- Start: run `git pull`, then read docs/STATUS.md before doing anything else.\n- End: update docs/STATUS.md (done, next, open questions), commit, and `git push`.\n- Never leave uncommitted work at the end of a session.\n","contentDiffersFromDisk":false},"displayPath":"CLAUDE.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"96236b88-5d3b-4b68-99c5-1a25d9f0ce0b","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:51:44.699Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/CLAUDE.md:\n\n# PFF Data Project — Agent Guide\n\nThis file is the canonical project memory for any AI coding agent working in this\nrepo. It is mirrored verbatim into `AGENTS.md` so OpenAI Codex (and any other\nagent that reads `AGENTS.md`) gets the same context. A pre-commit hook in\n`.githooks/pre-commit` blocks commits where the two files diverge.\n\nIf you edit one, edit both. See \"Keeping CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md in sync\" below.\n\n---\n\n## What this project is\n\nA standalone Python data pipeline that pulls Pro Football Focus (PFF) game and\nplayer data into a local **DuckDB** database for Baylor University Football\nanalysis. Scope is **Baylor + every Big 12 team + non-conference opponents** for\nthe **current season plus two prior**.\n\nThe DB *is* the deliverable. There are no dashboards in this repo. Downstream\nconsumers — Power BI, Jupyter notebooks, and a future athlete management system\n(AMS) project that will unify PFF with Catapult GPS, VALD ForceDecks, and Perch\nVBT data — own visualization and cross-domain joins.\n\n## Sister pipelines (do not modify from here)\n\n- **VALD ForceDecks (CMJ/IMTP):** `..\\..\\ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` — the\n  architectural template this project mirrors.\n- **Catapult GPS:** `..\\..\\DataBase_GPS_Reporting`\n- **Perch VBT:** not yet built.\n\nThe pattern across all of them is: per-domain Python pipeline → per-domain DB →\nfuture AMS layer unions them on `(athlete_id, source_system, pulled_at)`.\n\n## Tech stack\n\n- Python 3.12+\n- `duckdb`, `requests`, `python-dotenv`, `PyJWT`, `pyyaml`, `pandas`, `pytest`\n- No web framework, no ORM, no dashboard libs — keep it lean\n\n## Layout\n\n```\nPFF_Data_Project/\n├── CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md       # this file (mirrored)\n├── README.md                   # user-facing setup and usage\n├── requirements.txt\n├── .env.example                # PFF_BASE_URL, PFF_API_KEY, BAYLOR_TEAM_ID\n├── .gitignore\n├── .githooks/pre-commit        # blocks CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md divergence\n├── run_pipeline.py             # CLI entry\n├── config/\n│   └── pipeline.yaml           # team/season scope, endpoints, retry config\n├── src/\n│   ├── db.py                   # DuckDB schema + connect + backup helpers\n│   ├── ingest/\n│   │   ├── pff_client.py       # PFF HTTP client (stub until Phase 0 done)\n│   │   └── pipeline.py         # Orchestration: dims → facts → log\n│   └── shape/transform.py      # JSON → tidy rows (teams/games/grades/stats/premium)\n├── data/                       # small extracts only; the DuckDB lives at $PFF_DB\n├── docs/\n│   └── pff_api_notes.md        # Phase 0 spike output\n├── scripts/\n│   └── phase0_probe.py         # Credential discovery probe\n└── tests/\n    ├── test_db.py\n    └── fixtures/\n```\n\n## Data model contract\n\nDimensions are stable identities; facts are timestamped measurements.\n\n- **Dimensions:** `teams`, `players`, `player_seasons`, `games`\n- **Facts:** `pff_grades` (per-player per-game, long form), `pff_stats` (extended\n  stats, long form), `pff_premium` (advanced metrics, keyed JSON passthrough)\n- **Operational:** `ingest_log`\n\n`players` / `player_seasons` are derived from the grade rows (which carry\nplayer_id, name, position, team_id, season) — cleanest \"who actually played\"\nsource; avoids the giant all-NCAA `/players` pull.\n\nEvery fact row carries `source_system = 'PFF'` and `pulled_at`. This is\nnon-negotiable — it is the contract the future AMS layer relies on to union PFF\nwith Catapult, VALD, and Perch facts.\n\n`pff_player_id` is the durable join key. This pipeline does **not** maintain a\ncanonical athlete master record across systems. That mapping\n(`roster.athlete_id → pff_player_id`) belongs to the future AMS project. Do not\ninvent it here.\n\n## API contract (Phase 0 complete)\n\nFull contract is in `docs/pff_api_notes.md`; OpenAPI specs in `docs/swagger/`.\nEssentials:\n\n- **Auth:** `POST /auth/login` with header `x-api-key: <key>` → `{\"jwt\"}`. Send\n  `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` on all calls. JWT lasts ~1h; re-auth on 401.\n  Rate-limited (HTTP 429) — client backs off. `src/ingest/pff_client.py` owns this.\n- **Base:** `https://api.profootballfocus.com`, league slug `ncaa`, season = year.\n- **Baylor:** team_id / franchise_id = `118` (`BAYLOR_TEAM_ID` in `.env`).\n- **Scope is open:** the key reads all NCAA teams + grades for any game, so\n  Baylor + Big 12 + opponents is achievable despite the token's `franchise:118`\n  constraint.\n- **Play-by-play exists in the Video Feeds API** (EPA fields confirmed 2026-09-13):\n  `/v1/video/ncaa/games/{game_id}/plays`, 330+ fields per play, including PFF EPA\n  (`expected_points_added` and `expected_points_added_legacy`). Ingested for Baylor\n  2024-2025 into `pff_premium` as category `video_plays` by\n  `scripts/pull_video_plays.py`; deliberately not in `PREMIUM_CATEGORIES` (the\n  season loop would pull every scoped game at ~1.5 MB each).\n- **Full seasons stream from `/v1/video/ncaa/{season}/plays`** (~2.5 GB decoded,\n  about a minute): `scripts/pull_season_video_plays.py --season YYYY` writes\n  `data/video_plays_slim_<season>.parquet` (31 fields per play) and\n  `data/video_games_<season>.parquet` (final scores; the plays feed only has the\n  score before each snap). Both gitignored. `season_plays.to_frame` must build\n  the frame with `dtype=object`: without it an int column with a gap becomes\n  float64 and is stored as the text `\"2.0\"` and `\"nan\"`.\n  Premium feeds (`/v1/premium/...`) provide play-level advanced metrics → captured\n  in `pff_premium` as keyed JSON passthrough. All 12 API specs are in `docs/swagger/`.\n- **FUTURE:** player-tracking feeds are deliberately not modeled in v1.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Credentials never in code.** `.env` only. Loaded via `python-dotenv`. The\n  `.env` file is gitignored; `.env.example` is committed as the template.\n- **DuckDB upserts:** use `INSERT OR REPLACE INTO` (idempotent refresh — re-pulling\n  a week updates values rather than duplicating). See `_upsert` in `pipeline.py`.\n- **Config path resolution:** always anchor to `Path(__file__).resolve().parent`\n  walks, never relative paths from CWD. `run_pipeline.py` and `src/db.py` show\n  the pattern.\n- **The DB is not in the repo.** `src/db.py` resolves it from **`$PFF_DB`**, falling\n  back to the old `data/pff.db` when the variable is unset. Windows:\n  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\pff-data\\pff.db` (set 2026-09-22). Reason: `~/Desktop/DEV` is a\n  two-way Google Drive mirror, and Drive re-uploads and **locks** a 500 MB file on\n  every write; on 2026-09-22 it held the file open against a running pull\n  (`GoogleDriveFS.exe`). Keep the DB, its WAL and its backups out of the mirrored tree.\n- **Backups before destructive ops:** copy the DB to\n  `pff_before_<operation>_<YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS>.db` **beside it**, not into `data/`.\n  `src/db.backup_db()` does this — use it from any new destructive code path.\n- **Idempotent ingests:** running the same week twice must be a no-op.\n- **One concern per module.** Client does HTTP. Shape does JSON-to-rows. Pipeline\n  orchestrates. db.py owns the schema. Don't blur these.\n- **Tests use mocked HTTP**, not live API calls. The Phase 0 probe is the only\n  script that hits the real API.\n- **No comments that narrate the obvious.** Comments are for non-obvious WHYs\n  (e.g., \"DuckDB requires ON CONFLICT here; SQLite syntax silently fails\").\n\n## CLI commands\n\n```\npython run_pipeline.py --dry-run               # auth + scope check, no writes\npython run_pipeline.py --backfill              # full 3-season pull\npython run_pipeline.py --season 2025           # full season\npython run_pipeline.py --season 2025 --week 8  # one week\npython run_pipeline.py --diagnose [NAME]       # query local DB\npython run_pipeline.py --reset-db              # backup + reinit\n```\n\nMoving data between the Mac and Windows (never re-pull what the other machine already has):\n\n```\npython scripts/export_snapshot.py              # every season -> Parquet on Drive\npython scripts/export_snapshot.py --season 2026\npython scripts/load_snapshot.py --dry-run      # what would change here\npython scripts/load_snapshot.py                # load what Drive has\n```\n\n## Moving data between machines\n\nThe DB never travels. `git` cannot hold a 600 MB binary and a live database inside a\nsynced folder gets locked and re-uploaded on every write. Instead each machine keeps its\nown local DB and they exchange **immutable Parquet snapshots** through Google Drive:\nwritten once, never mutated, so a half-synced file cannot corrupt anything.\n\n- Root: `$PFF_SNAPSHOT_DIR`, else `.../00_Primary_Storage_Drive/pff-snapshots`.\n- One file per table per season (`pff_grades/season=2026.parquet`), so a single season\n  moves without dragging six million rows behind it. It is small: all of 2026,\n  1.33 M rows, is about 6 MB.\n- `manifest.json` records rows, bytes, which machine wrote it and when. An export\n  merges into it, so exporting one season never erases the record of the others.\n- The load is season-scoped and idempotent: it deletes and reinserts only the seasons\n  the snapshot carries, leaves the rest alone, and backs the DB up first.\n- `ingest_log` is never exported. It says what **this** machine fetched; copying it\n  would make the other machine's history a lie.\n- After `dev-sync` pulls the scripts, run `load_snapshot.py` to pull the data. Git\n  carries the code, Drive carries the rows.\n\n## Testing\n\n```\npytest tests/\n```\n\nTests that need a DB use a per-test temp path; don't touch `data/pff.db`.\n\n## Keeping CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md in sync\n\n`AGENTS.md` is a byte-identical copy of `CLAUDE.md`. The pre-commit hook at\n`.githooks/pre-commit` blocks commits when they differ. Activate the hook once\nper clone:\n\n```\ngit config core.hooksPath .githooks\n```\n\nWhen updating agent guidance, edit `CLAUDE.md` first, then:\n\n```\ncp CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md\ngit add CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md\n```\n\nIf you intentionally need them to diverge (tool-specific instructions for one\nagent), update the hook before committing — don't bypass it silently with\n`--no-verify`.\n\n## Working with this repo from Codex\n\nCodex reads `AGENTS.md` automatically. Everything above applies to Codex\nsessions identically. Codex-specific notes, if any are ever needed, go in a\nseparate section here and get mirrored.\n\n\n## Session routine\n- Start: run `git pull`, then read docs/STATUS.md before doing anything else.\n- End: update docs/STATUS.md (done, next, open questions), commit, and `git push`.\n- Never leave uncommitted work at the end of a session.\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"74f6814f-8ccf-42bc-aee0-c087dea07887","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project","sessionId":"74f6814f-8ccf-42bc-aee0-c087dea07887","version":"2.1.280","gitBranch":"main"}
